; ======================================================================
;  dsi_host.pi4 - the BCM2711's DSI1 host controller, its PHY, and the
;  three clocks that feed them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT THIS IS FOR. The firmware on this part lights a DSI panel only
;  for the one panel it has a driver for, and on the bench board it
;  lights none at all - `screen displays` answers 0 and there is no
;  MAIN_LCD. So everything a DSI panel needs has to be programmed here:
;  the link clock, the D-PHY, the escape-mode command path that carries
;  the panel's DCS init table, and then video mode.
;
;  THIS FILE IS THE SoC HALF ONLY. The panel's own registers - its power
;  rails through the MCU at I2C $45, and its 200-command DCS init table -
;  are dsi_panel_v2.pi4 and dsi_panel_v2_dcs.pi4. The split is on
;  purpose: everything here is true of any panel on DSI1, and nothing
;  here knows what is on the other end of the ribbon.
;
;  ---- USE ------------------------------------------------------------
;
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"     ; delay(), millis()
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"   ; the power domain
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dsi_host.pi4"
;
;    DsiDomainOn()                   ; FIRST. Nothing below moves without it
;    DsiClocksRead()                 ; what CPRMAN holds now - reads only
;    DsiModePlan(70000000)           ; pick the divider, adjust hblank
;    DsiClocksSet()                  ; program PLLD_DSI1, escape, pixel
;    DsiHostBringUp()                ; CTRL, AFE, the eighteen timings
;    ...the panel's DCS init table through DsiDcsWrite()...
;    DsiVideoOn()                    ; DISP0 ENABLE - pixels start
;
;  House rule 10: a library never includes a library. board.pi4 lists
;  timer.pi4 ahead of this file and a main file that forgets it fails
;  loudly at compile time on an undefined delay().
;
;  ---- WHERE EVERY NUMBER COMES FROM (do not guess - rule 21) ----------
;
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_vc4_dsi.c, the Linux driver for
;  this exact block, and the only published description of it. Broadcom
;  document nothing about DSI in the BCM2711 peripherals PDF. Cited
;  below as vc4_dsi.c:<line>.
;
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/rpi-6.12.y_vc4_regs.h and _vc4_drv.h for
;  VC4_SET_FIELD, which is FIELD_PREP: the shift comes from the MASK,
;  not from the _SHIFT constant beside it. Where the two disagree in the
;  header - and for DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_IDR_DLANE1 they DO, mask (27,23)
;  against a stated shift of 23, overlapping DLANE2's (28,26) - the MASK
;  is what the driver actually uses and what is reproduced here.
;
;  The clock manager's register map is not in the peripherals PDF
;  either. It comes from the downstream kernel's clock driver, fetched
;  2026-09-06 from
;  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raspberrypi/linux/rpi-6.12.y/
;  drivers/clk/bcm/clk-bcm2835.c
;  and cited below as clk-bcm2835.c:<line>. pwm.pi4 already uses the
;  same file for CM_PWMCTL, so this is a second consumer of a map this
;  tree already trusts.
;
;  ---- THE ONE THING THAT MUST NOT BE DONE, AND WHY -------------------
;
;  PLLD ITSELF IS NEVER TOUCHED. The driver says so in as many words at
;  vc4_dsi.c:851-857: "PLLD is set to 2Ghz, and may not be changed by the
;  display driver since most peripherals are hanging off of the PLLD_PER
;  divider." On this board PLLD_PER is what the UART, the SD host and
;  most of the rest are derived from, so moving PLLD would take the
;  console away mid-command and there would be no way to find out what
;  had happened.
;
;  What IS programmed here is the PLLD_DSI1 CHANNEL divider - an 8-bit
;  integer divider off PLLD's VCO that nothing else on the part reads -
;  and the two DSI1-only clocks in CPRMAN. So the blast radius of every
;  write in this file is DSI1. That is a much smaller claim than the
;  campaign plan's stage 5 made, and it is the reason this stage is safe
;  to run over a wireless console.
;
;  The price of not moving PLLD is that the link rate lands on whatever
;  an integer divider can reach, which is FASTER than the panel's stated
;  70 MHz pixel clock, and the horizontal blanking is stretched to put
;  the refresh rate back where it belongs. That is not a workaround
;  invented here; it is vc4_dsi_bridge_mode_fixup() (:864-915), and the
;  comment there says the firmware does the same thing.
;
;  ---- THE READ THAT IS NOT A READ ------------------------------------
;
;  DSI1_ID at offset $8C reads $00647369, ASCII "\0dsi". So does every
;  other unclaimed offset in the block - the driver says so at
;  vc4_dsi.c:530-532: "Actually, all registers in the range that aren't
;  otherwise claimed will return the ID." So reading the ID proves the
;  block answers and NOTHING ELSE. It cannot tell a live block from a
;  dead one, and a bring-up that stops there has measured nothing.
;
;  DsiHostProveWritable() is the honest version: write a value into a
;  CLAIMED register that has no side effects until video starts
;  (HS_DLT5's INIT field, 24 bits wide), read it back, and put the
;  driver's own value there afterwards. A block that is powered down
;  reads back zero; a block that is not there reads back the ID string.
;  Both are distinguishable from the value that was written.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DSI1 REGISTER BLOCK. Base from bcm283x.dtsi's dsi1@7e700000,
;  which is $FE700000 in the ARM's view of the peripherals on this part
;  (the same $7E -> $FE rebase every other file here uses).
;
;  OFFSETS ARE THE DSI1 ONES AND DSI0'S ARE DIFFERENT FOR THE SAME
;  REGISTER NAME. vc4_dsi.c carries two maps and picks between them with
;  DSI_PORT_*; getting this wrong reads STAT and writes PHYC. Every
;  offset below is from the DSI1 half, vc4_dsi.c:389-533.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSI1_BASE = $FE700000

#DSI1_CTRL_OFF           = $00
#DSI1_TXPKT1C_OFF        = $04
#DSI1_TXPKT1H_OFF        = $08
#DSI1_TXPKT2C_OFF        = $0C
#DSI1_TXPKT2H_OFF        = $10
#DSI1_RXPKT1H_OFF        = $14
#DSI1_RXPKT2H_OFF        = $18
#DSI1_TXPKT_CMD_FIFO_OFF = $1C
#DSI1_TXPKT_PIX_FIFO_OFF = $20
#DSI1_RXPKT_FIFO_OFF     = $24
#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF     = $28
#DSI1_DISP1_CTRL_OFF     = $2C
#DSI1_INT_STAT_OFF       = $30
#DSI1_INT_EN_OFF         = $34
#DSI1_STAT_OFF           = $38
#DSI1_HSTX_TO_CNT_OFF    = $3C
#DSI1_LPRX_TO_CNT_OFF    = $40
#DSI1_TA_TO_CNT_OFF      = $44
#DSI1_PR_TO_CNT_OFF      = $48
#DSI1_PHYC_OFF           = $4C
#DSI1_HS_CLT0_OFF        = $50
#DSI1_HS_CLT1_OFF        = $54
#DSI1_HS_CLT2_OFF        = $58
#DSI1_HS_DLT3_OFF        = $5C
#DSI1_HS_DLT4_OFF        = $60
#DSI1_HS_DLT5_OFF        = $64
#DSI1_HS_DLT6_OFF        = $68
#DSI1_HS_DLT7_OFF        = $6C
#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_OFF      = $70
#DSI1_PHY_AFEC1_OFF      = $74
#DSI1_TST_SEL_OFF        = $78
#DSI1_TST_MON_OFF        = $7C
#DSI1_PHY_TST1_OFF       = $80
#DSI1_PHY_TST2_OFF       = $84
#DSI1_PHY_FIFO_STAT_OFF  = $88
#DSI1_ID_OFF             = $8C

; vc4_dsi.c:387. ASCII "\0dsi", and see THE READ THAT IS NOT A READ.
#DSI_ID_VALUE = $00647369

; CTRL, vc4_dsi.c:389-425. The DSI1 spellings only.
#DSI_CTRL_EN                  = $0001   ; bit 0
#DSI_CTRL_DISABLE_DISP_ECCC   = $0002   ; bit 1
#DSI_CTRL_DISABLE_DISP_CRCC   = $0004   ; bit 2
#DSI_CTRL_CLR_CDF             = $0010   ; bit 4
#DSI_CTRL_CLR_PDF             = $0020   ; bit 5
#DSI_CTRL_CLR_RXF             = $0040   ; bit 6
#DSI_CTRL_CLR_LDF             = $0080   ; bit 7
#DSI_CTRL_INV_BYTE            = $0100   ; bit 8
#DSI_CTRL_CAL_BYTE            = $0200   ; bit 9
#DSI_CTRL_SOFT_RESET_CFG      = $0400   ; bit 10
#DSI_CTRL_HSDT_EOT_DISABLE    = $0800   ; bit 11
#DSI_CTRL_LPDT_EOT_DISABLE    = $1000   ; bit 12
#DSI_CTRL_RX_LPDT_EOT_DISABLE = $2000   ; bit 13

; DSI1_CTRL_RESET_FIFOS, vc4_dsi.c:422-425: CLR_LDF | CLR_RXF | CLR_PDF
; | CLR_CDF = $80 | $40 | $20 | $10. Written as the literal because a
; constant defined from other constants is refused by this compiler.
#DSI_CTRL_RESET_FIFOS = $00F0

; THE CTRL WORD THIS FILE MEANS, WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN READ BACK.
;
; vc4_dsi.c reaches CAL_BYTE with `READ(CTRL) | CAL_BYTE` (:1142-1144)
; and the FIFO reset with `READ(CTRL) | RESET_FIFOS` (:1397-1399). Both
; are read-modify-writes, and this block has already been caught holding
; a register that does NOT read back what was put in it: PHY_AFEC0,
; whose readable bits measured $0001B700 with every bias field gone.
;
; CTRL has never been proven to read back on this part. If it does not,
; `READ(CTRL) | CAL_BYTE` writes a word with DSI_CTRL_EN CLEARED - a
; disabled block, which would latch CMD_EN and do nothing with it, which
; is the observed symptom exactly. So the word is COMPOSED. It is the
; same value the read-modify-write produces on a register that reads
; back, and it is the right value on one that does not.
#DSI_CTRL_BASE    = $00002801   ; EN | HSDT_EOT_DISABLE | RX_LPDT_EOT_DISABLE
#DSI_CTRL_RUNNING = $00002A01   ; the above | CAL_BYTE
#DSI_CTRL_REFIFO  = $00002AF1   ; the above | RESET_FIFOS

; INT_STAT / INT_EN, DSI1 half, vc4_dsi.c:231-289.
#DSI1_INT_TXPKT1_END   = $00000001   ; bit 0
#DSI1_INT_TXPKT1_DONE  = $00000002   ; bit 1
#DSI1_INT_RXPKT1       = $00000010   ; bit 4
#DSI1_INT_ERR_SYNC_ESC = $00000040   ; bit 6
#DSI1_INT_ERR_CONTROL  = $00000080   ; bit 7
#DSI1_INT_ERR_CONT_LP0 = $00000100   ; bit 8
#DSI1_INT_ERR_CONT_LP1 = $00000200   ; bit 9
#DSI1_INT_HSTX_TO      = $00000400   ; bit 10
#DSI1_INT_LPRX_TO      = $00000800   ; bit 11
#DSI1_INT_TA_TO        = $00001000   ; bit 12
#DSI1_INT_PR_TO        = $00002000   ; bit 13
#DSI1_INT_PHY_DIR_FTR  = $00020000   ; bit 17
#DSI1_INT_PHY_DIR_RTF  = $00200000   ; bit 21
#DSI1_INT_FIFO_ERR     = $00400000   ; bit 22

; The eight bits the driver treats as errors, vc4_dsi.c:1609-1626:
; ERR_SYNC_ESC | ERR_CONTROL | ERR_CONT_LP0 | ERR_CONT_LP1 | HSTX_TO |
; LPRX_TO | TA_TO | PR_TO = $40|$80|$100|$200|$400|$800|$1000|$2000.
#DSI1_INT_ERROR_BITS = $00003FC0

; A transfer is finished when either of these appears: TXPKT1_DONE for a
; write, PHY_DIR_RTF for a read that has turned the bus around.
; vc4_dsi.c:1633-1637. $2 | $200000.
#DSI1_INT_XFER_DONE = $00200002

; STAT, DSI1 half, vc4_dsi.c:437-470. Only the ones this file reports.
#DSI1_STAT_TXPKT1_END   = $00000001   ; bit 0
#DSI1_STAT_TXPKT1_DONE  = $00000002   ; bit 1
#DSI1_STAT_TXPKT1_BUSY  = $00000004   ; bit 2
#DSI1_STAT_RXPKT1       = $00000040   ; bit 6
#DSI1_STAT_ERR_SYNC_ESC = $00000100   ; bit 8
#DSI1_STAT_ERR_CONTROL  = $00000200   ; bit 9
#DSI1_STAT_ERR_CONT_LP1 = $00000800   ; bit 11
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_CLOCK_STOP = $00010000 ; bit 16
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_CLOCK_HS   = $00020000 ; bit 17
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_D0_STOP    = $01000000 ; bit 24
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_D1_STOP    = $04000000 ; bit 26
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_CLOCK_ULPS = $00040000 ; bit 18
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_D0_ULPS    = $02000000 ; bit 25
#DSI1_STAT_PHY_D1_ULPS    = $08000000 ; bit 27

; The three STOP bits and the three ULPS bits for a two-lane link with a
; NON-CONTINUOUS clock, which is what this panel asks for. vc4_dsi.c
; builds exactly these two masks at :754-764.
#DSI1_STAT_STOP_2LANE = $05010000
#DSI1_STAT_ULPS_2LANE = $0A040000

; PHYC, DSI1 half, vc4_dsi.c:299-318.
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE0_ENABLE      = $00000001   ; bit 0
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE1_ENABLE      = $00000010   ; bit 4
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE2_ENABLE      = $00000100   ; bit 8
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE3_ENABLE      = $00001000   ; bit 12
#DSI1_PHYC_CLANE_ENABLE      = $00010000   ; bit 16
#DSI1_PHYC_HS_CLK_CONTINUOUS = $00040000   ; bit 18
#DSI1_PHYC_ESC_CLK_LPDT_SHIFT = 20         ; bits 25:20

; The ULPS REQUEST bits. These are how a lane is COMMANDED to change
; state, and they are the only documented way to make one move on
; demand. vc4_dsi.c:299-318: DLANE0_ULPS bit 1, DLANE1_ULPS bit 5,
; DSI1's CLANE_ULPS bit 17. The clock lane is included here because this
; panel runs a non-continuous clock, which is the same condition vc4
; uses at :746-752.
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE0_ULPS  = $00000002   ; bit 1
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE1_ULPS  = $00000020   ; bit 5
#DSI1_PHYC_CLANE_ULPS  = $00020000   ; bit 17
#DSI1_PHYC_ULPS_2LANE  = $00020022   ; the three above

; DLANE0_FORCE_STOP, vc4_dsi.c:316, bit 2. The reference driver never
; writes it. It is here because it is the most direct order this block
; accepts: not "negotiate a state change with the far end" but "put lane
; zero in stop", entirely inside the SoC. A lane that ignores THAT has
; nothing outside the part to blame.
#DSI_PHYC_DLANE0_FORCE_STOP = $00000004

; PHY_AFEC0, DSI1 half, vc4_dsi.c:328-374.
#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_RESET = $00002000   ; bit 13
#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_PD    = $00001000   ; bit 12

; DISP0_CTRL, vc4_dsi.c:135-167. Field positions are shared with DSI0.
#DSI_DISP0_ENABLE            = $0001   ; bit 0
#DSI_DISP0_COMMAND_MODE      = $0002   ; bit 1
#DSI_DISP0_PFORMAT_SHIFT     = 2       ; bits 3:2
#DSI_DISP0_ST_END            = $0010   ; bit 4
#DSI_DISP0_LP_STOP_CTRL_SHIFT = 11     ; bits 12:11
#DSI_DISP0_PIX_CLK_DIV_SHIFT  = 13     ; bits 21:13
#DSI_PFORMAT_RGB888          = 3
#DSI_DISP0_LP_STOP_PERFRAME  = 2

; DISP1_CTRL, vc4_dsi.c:168-184. DSI1's command FIFO is three bytes
; wide, so the 24-bit pixel format is the right one here - the 32-bit
; branch in the driver is DSI0's.
#DSI_DISP1_ENABLE            = $0001   ; bit 0
#DSI1_DISP1_PFORMAT_32BIT_LE = 2       ; bits 2:1, value 2
;
;  THE COMMAND FIFO ON THIS PART IS FOUR BYTES WIDE, NOT THREE, and the
;  first version of this file had it the other way round. The variant
;  table settles it: `bcm2711_dsi1_variant` has `.cmd_fifo_width = 4`
;  (vc4_dsi.c:1541-1547), and the driver picks the 32-bit little-endian
;  pixel format for exactly that case (:1155-1165). Three is the BCM2835
;  DSI0 figure.
;
;  It matters only for a LONG packet, whose tail goes through the pixel
;  FIFO, and this panel's table has none - so it is not the reason
;  nothing transmits. It is fixed because a wrong constant with a
;  citation next to it is worse than no constant at all.
#DSI1_DISP1_CTRL_VALUE       = $0005   ; (2 << 1) | ENABLE

; TXPKT1C, vc4_dsi.c:51-92.
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_EN          = $0001   ; bit 0
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_TE_EN       = $0002   ; bit 1
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_TYPE_LONG   = $0004   ; bit 2
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_MODE_LP     = $0008   ; bit 3
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_CTRL_SHIFT  = 4       ; bits 5:4
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_CTRL_TX     = 0
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_CTRL_RX     = 1
#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_REPEAT_SHIFT = 10     ; bits 23:10
#DSI_TXPKT1C_DISPLAY_NO_SHIFT = 8      ; bits 9:8, 0 = SHORT

; TXPKT1H, vc4_dsi.c:93-101: data type in 7:0, the two parameter bytes
; in 23:8, the command-FIFO byte count in 31:24.
#DSI_TXPKT1H_BC_PARAM_SHIFT   = 8
#DSI_TXPKT1H_BC_CMDFIFO_SHIFT = 24

; RXPKT1H, vc4_dsi.c:102-120.
#DSI_RXPKT1H_PKT_TYPE_LONG = $01000000   ; bit 24
#DSI_RXPKT1H_SHORT_0_SHIFT = 8           ; bits 15:8
#DSI_RXPKT1H_SHORT_1_SHIFT = 16          ; bits 23:16
#DSI_RXPKT1H_ERR_BITS      = $F0000000   ; CRC/DET/ECC/COR, bits 31:28

; The MIPI DSI data types this file sends. From include/video/mipi_display.h,
; which mipi_dsi_create_packet() maps message types onto; the three that a
; DCS init table and one status read need.
#MIPI_DSI_DCS_SHORT_WRITE       = $05   ; command, no parameter
#MIPI_DSI_DCS_SHORT_WRITE_PARAM = $15   ; command + one parameter
#MIPI_DSI_DCS_LONG_WRITE        = $39   ; command + two or more
#MIPI_DSI_DCS_READ              = $06   ; command, reply expected
#MIPI_DSI_SET_MAX_RETURN_SIZE   = $37

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CLOCK MANAGER. Base and password as pwm.pi4 already documents
;  them; the four offsets below are the DSI1 ones and are cited from the
;  clock driver because the peripherals PDF stops at the general-purpose
;  clocks.
;
;    clk-bcm2835.c:172  CM_PLLD    0x10c   the PLLD channel load/hold bits
;    clk-bcm2835.c:195  CM_DSI1ECTL 0x158  escape clock control
;    clk-bcm2835.c:196  CM_DSI1EDIV 0x15c  escape clock divider
;    clk-bcm2835.c:197  CM_DSI1PCTL 0x160  pixel clock control
;    clk-bcm2835.c:198  CM_DSI1PDIV 0x164  pixel clock divider
;    clk-bcm2835.c:208  A2W_PLLD_CTRL 0x1140  PLLD's NDIV and PDIV
;    clk-bcm2835.c:252  A2W_PLLD_FRAC 0x1240  PLLD's 20-bit fraction
;    clk-bcm2835.c:274  A2W_PLLD_PER  0x1540  the PER channel divider
;    clk-bcm2835.c:275  A2W_PLLD_DSI1 0x1640  the DSI1 channel divider
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSI_CM_BASE   = $FE101000
#DSI_CM_PASSWD = $5A000000

#CM_PLLD_OFF      = $010C
#CM_DSI1ECTL_OFF  = $0158
#CM_DSI1EDIV_OFF  = $015C
#CM_DSI1PCTL_OFF  = $0160
#CM_DSI1PDIV_OFF  = $0164
#A2W_PLLD_CTRL_OFF = $1140
#A2W_PLLD_FRAC_OFF = $1240
#A2W_PLLD_PER_OFF  = $1540
#A2W_PLLD_DSI1_OFF = $1640

; CM_PLLD's per-channel load and hold bits, clk-bcm2835.c:172-180.
#CM_PLLD_LOADDSI1 = $04   ; bit 2
#CM_PLLD_HOLDDSI1 = $08   ; bit 3

; A2W channel divider: eight bits of divider in 7:0 and a disable in
; bit 8. clk-bcm2835.c:258-260.
#A2W_PLL_CHANNEL_DISABLE = $0100

; PLLD's own control word, clk-bcm2835.c:211-216: NDIV in 9:0, PDIV in
; 14:12. The fraction register is twenty bits, :255-256.
#A2W_PLL_CTRL_NDIV_MASK  = $000003FF
#A2W_PLL_CTRL_PDIV_SHIFT = 12
#A2W_PLL_CTRL_PDIV_MASK  = $00007000
#A2W_PLL_FRAC_MASK       = $000FFFFF

; CM_*CTL bits, clk-bcm2835.c:124-136 - the same layout pwm.pi4 cites
; from Tables 99 and 100.
#CM_CTL_SRC_MASK = $0F
#CM_CTL_ENABLE   = $10   ; bit 4
#CM_CTL_KILL     = $20   ; bit 5
#CM_CTL_GATE     = $40   ; bit 6
#CM_CTL_BUSY     = $80   ; bit 7

; The source numbering, clk-bcm2835.c:137-150 for the general mux and
; :1710-1721 for DSI1's own. They are DIFFERENT muxes on the same field:
; a per-clock like the escape clock takes PLLD_PER at 6, while the DSI1
; pixel clock's mux is a list of the PHY's own outputs, and index 8 in
; THAT list is "dsi1_byte".
#CM_SRC_OSC      = 1
#CM_SRC_PLLD_PER = 6
#CM_SRC_DSI1_BYTE = 8

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CRYSTAL IS 54 MHz ON THIS PART, not 19.2. bcm2711.dtsi:618-620
;  overrides bcm283x.dtsi's 19200000, and this tree has measured the
;  same 54 MHz independently at CNTFRQ_EL0 (Lib/timer.pi4, 2026-08-24).
;  pwm.pi4 carries the same constant for the same reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSI_XOSC_HZ = 54000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PANEL'S MODE. 800x1280 portrait, from the driver's
;  ws_panel_10_1_a_mode (panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c:1812-1824), which is
;  the mode the "10_1_inch_a" descriptor names - the same string the
;  bench board's overlay line carries, and the same 10.1 inches the MCU
;  reports in its own size register.
;
;    clock 70000 kHz    hdisplay 800   hsync 840..860   htotal 880
;                       vdisplay 1280  vsync 1300..1320 vtotal 1324
;
;  70,000,000 / (880 * 1324) = 60.08 Hz.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSI_PANEL_HZ      = 70000000
#DSI_PANEL_HDISP   = 800
#DSI_PANEL_HSYNC_S = 840
#DSI_PANEL_HSYNC_E = 860
#DSI_PANEL_HTOTAL  = 880
#DSI_PANEL_VDISP   = 1280
#DSI_PANEL_VSYNC_S = 1300
#DSI_PANEL_VSYNC_E = 1320
#DSI_PANEL_VTOTAL  = 1324

; Two lanes, RGB888, so the bits-per-pixel-per-lane divider is 24/2.
; vc4_dsi.c:1451-1453; the panel descriptor's .lanes = 2 and
; .format = MIPI_DSI_FMT_RGB888 at panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c:2160-2162.
#DSI_LANES   = 2
#DSI_DIVIDER = 12

; Minimum LP state duration in escape-clock cycles: dsi_esc_timing(60)
; with a 10 ns escape period is 6. vc4_dsi.c:971 and :810-816.
#DSI_LPX = 6

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HOW LONG ONE TRANSFER IS ALLOWED, and why it is not the driver's 500.
;
;  The driver waits half a second, which is right for a kernel that has
;  something else to do meanwhile. This monitor does not: it polls, and
;  while it polls the wireless console is not serviced.
;
;  On 2026-09-07 that cost a hundred seconds of a board that answered
;  neither the console nor a ping, because two hundred DCS commands each
;  waited the full 500 ms. The board had not hung - its uptime went UP
;  across the episode - but there was no way to know that at the time,
;  and "unreachable" is what a hang looks like.
;
;  60 ms is still more than a hundred times the longest short packet at
;  this escape clock, so it cannot turn a slow success into a failure.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSI_XFER_MS = 60

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  STATE. Everything a probe would want to print after the fact, so that
;  a command can report what was programmed without re-reading hardware
;  that the act of reading might disturb.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global dsi_plld_vco.i      = 0    ; PLLD's VCO rate in Hz, as read
Global dsi_plld_per.i      = 0    ; the PER channel's rate in Hz
Global dsi_chan_div.i      = 0    ; the PLLD_DSI1 divider now in the part
Global dsi_pll_div.i       = 0    ; the divider this file chose
Global dsi_phy_hz.i        = 0    ; the link rate that divider gives
Global dsi_pixel_hz.i      = 0    ; phy / 12, the real pixel clock
Global dsi_esc_hz.i        = 0    ; the escape clock as programmed
Global dsi_htotal.i        = 0    ; the stretched horizontal total
Global dsi_hsync_s.i       = 0
Global dsi_hsync_e.i       = 0
Global dsi_planned.i       = 0    ; 1 once DsiModePlan has run
Global dsi_up.i            = 0    ; 1 once DsiHostBringUp has run
Global dsi_video.i         = 0    ; 1 once DISP0_ENABLE is set
Global dsi_xfers.i         = 0    ; DCS transfers attempted
Global dsi_xfer_fails.i    = 0    ; and how many did not complete
Global dsi_last_int.i      = 0    ; INT_STAT at the end of the last one
Global dsi_last_stat.i     = 0    ; and STAT, which no enable mask gates
Global dsi_last_rxpkt.i    = 0    ; RXPKT1H after the last read
Global dsi_afec0_want.i    = 0    ; the AFE word that was composed
Global dsi_afec0_seen.i    = 0    ; and what came back IMMEDIATELY after
                                  ; it was written - see step 4 below
Global dsi_ctrl_seen.i     = 0    ; CTRL read back one instruction after
                                  ; the composed word was written to it
Global dsi_stat_release.i  = 0    ; STAT 20 ms after the AFE left reset,
                                  ; with the clear taken BEFORE the
                                  ; release - see step 10 below
Global dsi_stat_settled.i  = 0    ; and STAT over a second 20 ms window,
                                  ; cleared after the release, which is
                                  ; what the old code measured alone
Global dsi_ulps_ran.i      = 0    ; 1 once DsiUlpsCycle has been run
Global dsi_ulps_enter.i    = 0    ; STAT after the ULPS request, or -1
Global dsi_ulps_exit.i     = 0    ; STAT after the request was removed
Global dsi_ulps_rc.i        = 0   ; 0 neither, 1 entered, 2 both
Global dsi_fs_ran.i        = 0    ; 1 once DsiForceStopCycle has been run
Global dsi_fs_phyc_was.i   = 0    ; PHYC before the order
Global dsi_fs_phyc_back.i  = 0    ; PHYC read back WITH the order in it
Global dsi_fs_stat.i       = 0    ; STAT after waiting for D0_STOP
Global dsi_fs_rc.i         = 0    ; 1 the lane reported STOP, 0 it did not

; ======================================================================
;  THE FIRMWARE POWER DOMAIN, AND IT IS THE FIRST THING THAT HAS TO
;  HAPPEN. Measured on the bench 2026-09-08.
;
;  DSI1's ANALOGUE front end sits in a power domain the VideoCore
;  firmware owns, and that domain is OFF at boot. Until it is turned on,
;  every register in this file reads back exactly the value written to
;  it and NOTHING MOVES: no lane reaches STOP, ULPS is neither entered
;  nor left, a DCS read gets no byte back. The digital register file
;  answers from a different power island than the analogue drive, so a
;  complete, correct bring-up verified write-by-write against the vendor
;  driver drives nothing at all. That cost two days before it was found.
;
;  THE DRIVER ASKS BEFORE IT WRITES: vc4_dsi.c:937,
;  pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev) is the FIRST statement of
;  vc4_dsi_bridge_pre_enable, ahead of every register write.
;
;  THE INDEX IS 19, NOT 18. The device-tree binding index and the
;  firmware's own index are not the same number:
;    bcm2835-rpi.dtsi:82-84   &dsi1 power-domains = <&power RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_DSI1>
;    raspberrypi-power.h:30   RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_DSI1  18
;    raspberrypi-power.h:9-11 "the firmware interface's indices minus one"
;    raspberrypi-power.c:120  dom->domain = xlate_index + 1     ->  19
;
;  AND A ZERO IS NOT NECESSARILY AN ANSWER. raspberrypi-power.c:137-159:
;  "The firmware doesn't actually return an error on an unknown tag, and
;  just skips over it" - so a firmware that has never heard of this tag
;  hands the buffer back unchanged, and an untouched state word of 0
;  looks exactly like a domain that is genuinely off. DsiDomainProbe()
;  below is the kernel's own test for that, and it lives here rather
;  than in a command file because everything that depends on the domain
;  has to be able to ask.
;
;  WHY THIS IS IN THE LIBRARY AND NOT IN THE `dsi domain` COMMAND, WHICH
;  IS WHERE IT WAS FIRST WRITTEN. A console command is something a
;  person types. The boot path cannot type, and a boot path that
;  reimplemented the message would be a second implementation of the one
;  thing this whole campaign turned on. The command is now a PRINTER
;  over these procedures: it reports the words they recorded, and the
;  words are composed once.
;
;  REQUIRES mailbox.pi4, ahead of this file in the main source. House
;  rule 10: this library does not include it, and a main file that
;  forgets it fails loudly on an undefined MailboxBegin.
; ======================================================================

; The four tags. raspberrypi-firmware-soc.h's tag enum.
#DSI_TAG_GET_POWER_STATE  = $00020001
#DSI_TAG_SET_POWER_STATE  = $00028001
#DSI_TAG_GET_DOMAIN_STATE = $00030030
#DSI_TAG_SET_DOMAIN_STATE = $00038030

; Firmware domain indices = binding index + 1. See above.
#DSI_DOMAIN_DSI0 = 18   ; RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_DSI0 17, plus one
#DSI_DOMAIN_DSI1 = 19   ; RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_DSI1 18, plus one
#DSI_DOMAIN_ARM  = 23   ; RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_ARM  22, plus one

; The number the kernel's implemented-or-not probe ACTUALLY sends. It is
; the binding index with no `+ 1` - raspberrypi-power.c:151 against :120,
; which is an inconsistency in the kernel and not in this transcription.
; Only used as a fallback by the probe, never by a SET.
#DSI_DOMAIN_ARM_BINDING = 22

; The OLD interface takes DEVICE ids, which are a different table
; entirely. raspberrypi-firmware-soc.h does NOT list them and there is no
; DSI device in it - only raspberrypi-power.c's two survivors, USB 3 and
; V3D 10, are named anywhere on this disk. So there is no old-interface
; call to make for DSI1, and nothing here invents one. Device 3 is READ,
; never written, purely to prove the device path answers at all -
; without that control, a silent DSI answer and a dead tag look alike.
#DSI_OLD_DEVICE_USB = 3

#DSI_MBX_TWO_WORDS = 8
#DSI_MBX_STATE_ON  = 1

; The last transaction, kept so a command file can PRINT every word that
; went out and came back without composing the message a second time.
Global dsi_dom_tag.i   = 0
Global dsi_dom_id.i    = 0
Global dsi_dom_req.i   = 0    ; the state word actually sent
Global dsi_dom_w0.i    = 0    ; the reply's word 0
Global dsi_dom_w1.i    = 0    ; and word 1
Global dsi_dom_nb.i    = 0    ; response bytes
Global dsi_dom_on.i    = 0    ; 1 once DSI1's domain has READ BACK on

; ======================================================================
;  THE TWO ACCESSORS. Everything in this file goes through them, so
;  there is one place a barrier can be missing from.
;
;  PeekN reads FOUR bytes - one 32-bit load, which is what a peripheral
;  register wants. The mask is not decoration: a register with bit 31
;  set arrives sign-extended and every comparison after it is wrong.
; ======================================================================
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiDomainXact - ONE power-domain mailbox transaction, and the only
;  place one is composed. Returns the reply's state word, or -1 if the
;  message could not be sent at all. Every word of it - the tag, the id,
;  what was sent, what came back and how many bytes came back - is left
;  in the dsi_dom_* globals for whoever wants to print it.
;
;  sendState = 0 means "send zero as the state word", which is what a
;  plain GET does; 1 means send `state` as given, which both a SET and
;  the seeded ~0 probe need.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiDomainXact(tag.i, id.i, state.i, sendState.i)
  dsi_dom_tag = tag
  dsi_dom_id  = id
  dsi_dom_req = 0
  If sendState <> 0
    dsi_dom_req = state
  EndIf
  dsi_dom_w0 = 0
  dsi_dom_w1 = 0
  dsi_dom_nb = 0

  If MailboxReady() = 0
    If MailboxInit() = 0
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If MailboxBegin(tag, #DSI_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #DSI_MBX_TWO_WORDS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, id)
  MailboxSetWord(1, dsi_dom_req)
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  dsi_dom_w0 = MailboxWord(0)
  dsi_dom_w1 = MailboxWord(1)
  dsi_dom_nb = MailboxRespBytes()
  ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_w1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiDomainGet - the state of one firmware power domain, or -1 if the
;  question could not be asked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiDomainGet(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn DsiDomainXact(#DSI_TAG_GET_DOMAIN_STATE, id, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiDomainProbe - IS THIS INTERFACE IMPLEMENTED AT ALL?
;
;  raspberrypi-power.c:137-159's own test, not an invention of this
;  bench: ask for a domain that must be on, with the state word
;  pre-seeded to $FFFFFFFF, and see whether the word comes back
;  DIFFERENT. A firmware that skipped the tag hands back what it was
;  given. The ARM domain is the one asked, because this board is running
;  on it, so any firmware maintaining the table at all has to say 1.
;
;  Returns 1 implemented, 0 not, -1 the message would not go.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiDomainProbe()
  Define v.i

  v = DsiDomainXact(#DSI_TAG_GET_DOMAIN_STATE, #DSI_DOMAIN_ARM, $FFFFFFFF, 1)
  If v < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If v <> $FFFFFFFF
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ; Unchanged at firmware index 23. Before calling a whole interface
  ; absent, ask the number the kernel's own probe sends: :151 sends the
  ; BINDING index 22, without the +1 it applies at :120. If 23 is past
  ; the end of this firmware's table, an unanswered word is exactly what
  ; that looks like.
  v = DsiDomainXact(#DSI_TAG_GET_DOMAIN_STATE, #DSI_DOMAIN_ARM_BINDING, $FFFFFFFF, 1)
  If v < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If v <> $FFFFFFFF
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiDomainOn - POWER DSI1, and prove it by reading it back.
;
;  This is the one call the boot path makes. It returns 1 only when the
;  domain READS BACK on, never on the strength of the SET's own reply:
;  the reply to a skipped tag is the request handed back, so a SET that
;  "succeeded" against a firmware which ignored it is indistinguishable
;  from one that worked, and only the read-back separates them.
;
;  It is idempotent and cheap - one mailbox round trip when the domain
;  is already known on - so anything that needs the block powered can
;  simply call it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiDomainOn()
  Define v.i

  If dsi_dom_on <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If DsiDomainXact(#DSI_TAG_SET_DOMAIN_STATE, #DSI_DOMAIN_DSI1, #DSI_MBX_STATE_ON, 1) < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  v = DsiDomainGet(#DSI_DOMAIN_DSI1)
  If v = 1
    dsi_dom_on = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DsiDomainIsOn() : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_on : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiDomTag()     : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_tag : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiDomId()      : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_id  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiDomReq()     : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_req : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiDomW0()      : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_w0  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiDomW1()      : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_w1  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i DsiDomBytes()   : ProcedureReturn dsi_dom_nb  : EndProcedure

Procedure.i DsiRd(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#DSI1_BASE + off) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure DsiWr(off.i, v.i)
  PokeN(#DSI1_BASE + off, v & $FFFFFFFF)
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DsiCmRd(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#DSI_CM_BASE + off) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; Every clock-manager write carries the password in 31:24 or is
; discarded silently. One procedure so it cannot be forgotten at a call
; site - the argument is the VALUE and the password is added here.
;
; NOTE the width: pwm.pi4's equivalent masks the value to 24 bits
; because CM_*CTL and CM_*DIV have nothing above bit 23. The A2W
; registers DO - the channel divider's disable bit is bit 8 and the PLL
; control word runs to bit 14 - but nothing this file writes goes above
; bit 23 either, so the same mask holds and is kept for the same reason.
Procedure DsiCmWr(off.i, v.i)
  PokeN(#DSI_CM_BASE + off, #DSI_CM_PASSWD | (v & $00FFFFFF))
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CLOCKS
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiPllVcoHz() - PLLD's VCO rate, computed from what the part holds.
;
;  bcm2835_pll_get_rate (clk-bcm2835.c:592-620) with
;  bcm2835_pll_rate_from_divisors (:565-577):
;
;      rate = parent * ((ndiv << 20) + fdiv) / pdiv >> 20
;
;  THE PRE-DIVIDER IS NOT READ, and that is correct on this part rather
;  than a shortcut. bcm2835_pll_get_prediv_mask (:520-533) returns 0 for
;  SOC_BCM2711 with the comment "On BCM2711 there isn't a pre-divisor
;  available in the PLL feedback loop. Bits 13:14 of ANA1 have been
;  re-purposed for VCO RANGE bits." Reading ANA1 here and doubling ndiv
;  on a set bit would halve the answer on a part where those bits mean
;  something else entirely.
;
;  Returns 0 if PDIV reads zero, which the driver also treats as "no
;  rate" rather than dividing by it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiPllVcoHz()
  Define ctl.i
  Define ndiv.i
  Define pdiv.i
  Define fdiv.i
  Define whole.i
  Define frac.i

  ctl  = DsiCmRd(#A2W_PLLD_CTRL_OFF)
  fdiv = DsiCmRd(#A2W_PLLD_FRAC_OFF) & #A2W_PLL_FRAC_MASK
  ndiv = ctl & #A2W_PLL_CTRL_NDIV_MASK
  pdiv = (ctl & #A2W_PLL_CTRL_PDIV_MASK) >> #A2W_PLL_CTRL_PDIV_SHIFT

  If pdiv = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Split so the intermediate stays far away from the top of a signed
  ; 64-bit integer. 54e6 * 1023 is 5.5e10 and 54e6 * fdiv is at most
  ; 5.7e13, where the single-expression form would reach 5.8e16 - safe
  ; today and one wider PLL away from not being.
  whole = (#DSI_XOSC_HZ * ndiv) / pdiv
  frac  = ((#DSI_XOSC_HZ * fdiv) / pdiv) >> 20
  ProcedureReturn whole + frac
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiClocksRead() - what CPRMAN holds RIGHT NOW. Reads only; writes
;  nothing; safe on a board whose display state is unknown, which is the
;  whole point of having it separate from DsiClocksSet().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DsiClocksRead()
  Define perdiv.i

  dsi_plld_vco = DsiPllVcoHz()

  perdiv = DsiCmRd(#A2W_PLLD_PER_OFF) & $FF
  If perdiv = 0
    ; An eight-bit field reading zero means 256 in this part's dividers
    ; (clk-bcm2835.c:876-878 folds max_div back to 0 on the way in).
    perdiv = 256
  EndIf
  dsi_plld_per = dsi_plld_vco / perdiv

  dsi_chan_div = DsiCmRd(#A2W_PLLD_DSI1_OFF) & $FF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiModePlan(pixelHz) - choose the PLLD_DSI1 divider and stretch the
;  horizontal blanking to keep the refresh rate.
;
;  This is vc4_dsi_bridge_mode_fixup (vc4_dsi.c:864-915), transcribed.
;  The loop finds the largest divider whose NEXT step would fall below
;  the wanted link rate - so the rate it picks is at or above what was
;  asked for, never below. A DSI link that is slightly fast with the
;  blanking stretched to match shows a correct picture; one that is slow
;  underruns the FIFO and tears.
;
;  Sets the globals and returns the pixel clock it landed on, or 0 if
;  PLLD could not be read at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiModePlan(pixelHz.i)
  Define want.i
  Define parent.i
  Define d.i
  Define pll.i

  DsiClocksRead()
  parent = dsi_plld_vco
  If parent = 0
    dsi_planned = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  want = pixelHz * #DSI_DIVIDER

  d = 1
  While d < 255
    If (parent / (d + 1)) < want
      Break
    EndIf
    d = d + 1
  Wend

  pll = parent / d

  dsi_pll_div  = d
  dsi_phy_hz   = pll
  dsi_pixel_hz = pll / #DSI_DIVIDER

  ; The blanking stretch, :908-913. The driver works in kHz because a
  ; drm mode's clock is in kHz; this works in Hz, which is the same
  ; arithmetic with the rounding step removed.
  dsi_htotal  = (dsi_pixel_hz * #DSI_PANEL_HTOTAL) / #DSI_PANEL_HZ
  dsi_hsync_s = #DSI_PANEL_HSYNC_S + (dsi_htotal - #DSI_PANEL_HTOTAL)
  dsi_hsync_e = #DSI_PANEL_HSYNC_E + (dsi_htotal - #DSI_PANEL_HTOTAL)

  dsi_planned = 1
  ProcedureReturn dsi_pixel_hz
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiCmStop(ctl) / DsiCmStart(ctl, src, div) - a CPRMAN clock generator,
;  stopped and started the way bcm2835_clock_off/on do it
;  (clk-bcm2835.c:1096-1130).
;
;  THE WAIT ON BUSY IS NOT POLITENESS. Table 99 on ENAB: the output
;  clock does not stop immediately because the cycle must complete, and
;  BUSY goes low when the final one has. Changing SRC or DIV while BUSY
;  is set glitches the output, and on a clock feeding a PHY that is a
;  glitch nothing downstream reports.
;
;  The wait is bounded. A clock that never goes idle is a fault to
;  report, not a reason to spin at a prompt for ever.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiCmStop(ctlOff.i)
  Define spin.i
  Define c.i

  c = DsiCmRd(ctlOff)
  DsiCmWr(ctlOff, c & ~(#CM_CTL_ENABLE | #CM_CTL_GATE))

  spin = 100000
  While spin > 0
    If (DsiCmRd(ctlOff) & #CM_CTL_BUSY) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    spin = spin - 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure DsiCmStart(ctlOff.i, divOff.i, src.i, div.i)
  DsiCmWr(divOff, div)
  DsiCmWr(ctlOff, src & #CM_CTL_SRC_MASK)
  DsiCmWr(ctlOff, (src & #CM_CTL_SRC_MASK) | #CM_CTL_ENABLE | #CM_CTL_GATE)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiClocksSet() - program the three clocks DSI1 needs.
;
;  ONE: the link clock, PLLD_DSI1. bcm2835_pll_divider_set_rate
;  (clk-bcm2835.c:866-890) writes the raw divider to the A2W channel
;  register - which also clears the channel's disable bit, because that
;  bit is bit 8 and a divider under 256 does not reach it - and then
;  pulses LOADDSI1 in CM_PLLD. bcm2835_pll_divider_on (:824-840) then
;  clears the channel disable and the HOLD bit.
;
;  TWO: the escape clock. vc4_dsi.c only enables it and never sets its
;  rate, because on a Linux system the firmware has already put it at
;  100 MHz - and the driver's own timing arithmetic is written around
;  that: "ESC always runs at 100Mhz" (:810-811) with ESC_TIME_NS 10.
;  Nothing has configured it on a board where the firmware owns no
;  display, so it is programmed here, from PLLD_PER, and the rate it
;  actually reached is reported rather than assumed.
;
;  THREE: the pixel clock, DSI1P. It has no divider at all - int_bits
;  and frac_bits are both zero (clk-bcm2835.c:2273-2280) - so it is a
;  pure mux, and setting its "rate" means choosing which of the PHY's
;  own outputs to take. The driver asks for phy/8, which is the BYTE
;  clock, and spends a paragraph on why it is the byte rate and not the
;  pixel rate (vc4_dsi.c:1052-1058).
;
;  Returns 1 if every step completed, 0 if a clock would not go idle.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiClocksSet()
  Define cm.i
  Define fixed.i
  Define escdiv.i
  Define ok.i

  If dsi_planned = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ok = 1

  ; ---- the link clock ------------------------------------------------
  DsiCmWr(#A2W_PLLD_DSI1_OFF, dsi_pll_div & $FF)
  cm = DsiCmRd(#CM_PLLD_OFF)
  DsiCmWr(#CM_PLLD_OFF, cm | #CM_PLLD_LOADDSI1)
  DsiCmWr(#CM_PLLD_OFF, cm & ~#CM_PLLD_LOADDSI1)

  DsiCmWr(#A2W_PLLD_DSI1_OFF, dsi_pll_div & $FF)   ; channel disable clear
  cm = DsiCmRd(#CM_PLLD_OFF)
  DsiCmWr(#CM_PLLD_OFF, cm & ~#CM_PLLD_HOLDDSI1)

  dsi_chan_div = DsiCmRd(#A2W_PLLD_DSI1_OFF) & $FF

  ; ---- the escape clock ----------------------------------------------
  ; bcm2835_clock_choose_div (clk-bcm2835.c:900-940): the divider is a
  ; 12-bit fixed point, integer in 23:12 and fraction in 11:0, of which
  ; only the top frac_bits are used - four integer bits and eight
  ; fractional ones for this clock (:2257-2264). So the unused low four
  ; bits are cleared, exactly as the driver's unused_frac_mask does.
  If dsi_plld_per > 0
    fixed = (dsi_plld_per << 12) / 100000000
    fixed = fixed & $0000FFF0
    If fixed < $1000
      fixed = $1000          ; never divide by less than one
    EndIf
    If fixed > $F000
      fixed = $F000          ; four integer bits is the field's whole width
    EndIf
    escdiv = fixed
    dsi_esc_hz = (dsi_plld_per << 12) / escdiv
    If DsiCmStop(#CM_DSI1ECTL_OFF) = 0
      ok = 0
    EndIf
    DsiCmStart(#CM_DSI1ECTL_OFF, #CM_DSI1EDIV_OFF, #CM_SRC_PLLD_PER, escdiv)
  Else
    dsi_esc_hz = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- the pixel clock -----------------------------------------------
  If DsiCmStop(#CM_DSI1PCTL_OFF) = 0
    ok = 0
  EndIf
  DsiCmStart(#CM_DSI1PCTL_OFF, #CM_DSI1PDIV_OFF, #CM_SRC_DSI1_BYTE, 0)

  ProcedureReturn ok
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE PHY TIMINGS
;
;  dsi_hs_timing(ui_ns, ns, ui) = roundup(ui + ceil(ns / ui_ns), 8),
;  vc4_dsi.c:800-807, rounded up to a multiple of eight "because we're
;  using the byte clock".
;
;  ui_ns is how many nanoseconds one unit interval is, and it is
;  DIV_ROUND_UP(500000000, hs_clock) - five hundred million rather than
;  a thousand million because the clock is DDR, so there is an extra
;  divide by two folded in (:1064-1067).
;
;  At any link rate this bench will use, ui_ns rounds up to 1, which
;  makes every `ns` term equal to itself. The arithmetic is written out
;  in full anyway rather than folded to constants: a panel with a slower
;  link would give ui_ns = 2 and every one of these numbers would change.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i DsiUiNs(hsClock.i)
  If hsClock <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (500000000 + hsClock - 1) / hsClock
EndProcedure

Procedure.i DsiHsTiming(uiNs.i, ns.i, ui.i)
  Define v.i
  If uiNs <= 0
    uiNs = 1
  EndIf
  v = ui + ((ns + uiNs - 1) / uiNs)
  ProcedureReturn ((v + 7) / 8) * 8
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiHostProveWritable() - the readback the block cannot fake.
;
;  Reading DSI1_ID proves nothing (see the header). This writes a
;  pattern into HS_DLT5's 24-bit INIT field, reads it back, restores
;  whatever was there, and returns 1 only if the value came back.
;
;  HS_DLT5 is chosen because it is a claimed register with no effect
;  until the PHY starts a transfer, so a wrong value here cannot damage
;  anything and does not survive DsiHostBringUp(), which writes the
;  driver's own value over it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiHostProveWritable()
  Define was.i
  Define got.i

  was = DsiRd(#DSI1_HS_DLT5_OFF)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT5_OFF, $00A5C300)
  got = DsiRd(#DSI1_HS_DLT5_OFF)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT5_OFF, was)

  If got = $00A5C300
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiHostBringUp() - vc4_dsi_bridge_pre_enable (vc4_dsi.c:917-1190),
;  transcribed, minus the two pieces that do not apply.
;
;  WHAT IS LEFT OUT, and why each is safe to leave out:
;
;  * The interrupt enables. Every one of them is there so that a Linux
;    completion can be woken; this file polls INT_STAT instead and the
;    same bits are just as readable from the ARM. Nothing else in the
;    monitor wants a DSI interrupt.
;
;  * vc4_dsi_ulps(dsi, false) at :1177. It IS a no-op on this path, and
;    the transcription that left it out was right about the outcome and
;    wrong about the reason, which matters enough to correct here.
;
;    THE OLD REASON WAS VOID. It said the call returns immediately
;    because PHY_AFEC0's LATCH_ULPS bit is left clear four steps
;    earlier - i.e. it rested on READING that bit. LATCH_ULPS is bit 14
;    and this part's PHY_AFEC0 has measured readable bits of $0001B700,
;    which does not include bit 14. The bit cannot be read at all. The
;    conclusion happens to hold - an unreadable bit reads zero, so the
;    driver's `ulps == ulps_currently_enabled` test is satisfied and it
;    returns - but it holds because the readback is BROKEN, not because
;    the state was known.
;
;    The consequence is the part worth keeping: on this part NOTHING in
;    either driver ever commands a lane to change state. vc4's ULPS exit
;    is dead code here. So DsiUlpsCycle() below exists as an instrument -
;    it does the enter-then-exit explicitly, without consulting the bit
;    that cannot be read, and reports what STAT says at each end. That is
;    the only documented way to ask the D-PHY's lane state machines
;    whether they respond to anything at all.
;
;  THE ORDER IS NOT NEGOTIABLE and the driver's comments say why at each
;  step; the numbering below matches the campaign plan's stage-3 list so
;  a reader can check one against the other.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiHostBringUp()
  Define uiNs.i
  Define afec0.i
  Define phyc.i

  If dsi_planned = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; 1. The block must be ENABLED for the FIFO resets to trigger - that
  ;    is the driver's own comment at :986-988, and it is why this write
  ;    and the next one are not one write.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_CTRL_OFF, #DSI_CTRL_SOFT_RESET_CFG | #DSI_CTRL_RESET_FIFOS)

  ; 2. Enable, with both end-of-transmission packets suppressed.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_CTRL_OFF, #DSI_CTRL_BASE)
  dsi_ctrl_seen = DsiRd(#DSI1_CTRL_OFF)

  ; 3. Clear every sticky status bit, so that what appears afterwards is
  ;    attributable to this bring-up and not to whatever ran last.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF))

  ; 4. The analogue front end. The composed value is
  ;    PTATADJ 7 | CTATADJ 7 | IDR 6 on the clock lane and all four data
  ;    lanes, the unused lanes powered down, and RESET held.
  ;
  ;    Every field is placed with its MASK's low bit, which is what
  ;    FIELD_PREP does, and for IDR_DLANE1 that mask is (27,23) even
  ;    though the header's _SHIFT beside it says the same 23 while
  ;    overlapping DLANE2's (28,26). The masked result is what the
  ;    driver writes, so it is what is written here.
  afec0 = (7 << 4) | 7
  afec0 = afec0 | (6 << 17)                    ; IDR_CLANE,  mask 19:17
  afec0 = afec0 | (6 << 20)                    ; IDR_DLANE0, mask 22:20
  afec0 = afec0 | ((6 << 23) & $0F800000)      ; IDR_DLANE1, mask 27:23
  afec0 = afec0 | ((6 << 26) & $1C000000)      ; IDR_DLANE2, mask 28:26
  afec0 = afec0 | ((6 << 29) & $E0000000)      ; IDR_DLANE3, mask 31:29
  afec0 = afec0 | $00000100                    ; PD_DLANE3 - two lanes
  afec0 = afec0 | $00000200                    ; PD_DLANE2 - two lanes
  afec0 = afec0 | #DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_RESET
  dsi_afec0_want = afec0
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_OFF, afec0)

  ; READ IT STRAIGHT BACK, AND KEEP BOTH NUMBERS.
  ;
  ; On 2026-09-07 this register held $00000300 after the whole bring-up
  ; - the two lane power-downs and nothing else - with every bias and
  ; drive field zero, and the PHY then refused to start a single packet.
  ; There are two ways that can happen and they need different fixes:
  ; the write did not take, or the fields do not read back and step 10's
  ; read-modify-write wiped them. Storing what the register says HERE,
  ; one instruction after the write, tells the two apart instead of
  ; leaving it to be argued about.
  dsi_afec0_seen = DsiRd(#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_OFF)

  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHY_AFEC1_OFF, 0)
  delay(1)                                     ; AFEC reset hold time

  ; 5. The eighteen timing fields. hs_clock is the link rate the divider
  ;    actually gave, not the one that was asked for.
  uiNs = DsiUiNs(dsi_phy_hz)

  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_CLT0_OFF, (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 262, 0) << 18) | (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 0, 8) << 9) | DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 38, 0))
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_CLT1_OFF, (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 60, 0) << 9) | DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 60, 52))
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_CLT2_OFF, DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 1000000, 0))
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT3_OFF, (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 100, 0) << 18) | (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 105, 6) << 9) | DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 40, 4))

  ; HS_DLT4's TRAIL is the larger of two candidates, :1099-1102, and the
  ; ANLAT field is a literal zero in the driver.
  If DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 0, 8) > DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 60, 4)
    DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT4_OFF, (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 0, 8) << 9) | DsiHsTiming(uiNs, #DSI_LPX * 10, 0))
  Else
    DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT4_OFF, (DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 60, 4) << 9) | DsiHsTiming(uiNs, #DSI_LPX * 10, 0))
  EndIf

  ; T_INIT: five milliseconds of STOP after power-up. The D-PHY spec
  ; asks 100 us; the driver keeps the firmware's "extremely conservative
  ; 5ms" (:1103-1110) and so does this. Do not optimise it on a first
  ; bring-up - it is the window the panel uses to notice the master.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT5_OFF, DsiHsTiming(uiNs, 5000000, 0))

  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT6_OFF, ((#DSI_LPX * 5) << 24) | (#DSI_LPX << 16) | ((#DSI_LPX * 4) << 8) | #DSI_LPX)

  ; LP_WUP is escape-clock timing, not HS timing: 1 ms at 10 ns a tick.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HS_DLT7_OFF, 100000)

  ; 6. The lanes. Two data lanes and the clock lane, and NO continuous
  ;    clock, because this panel asks for MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS
  ;    (panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c:2159-2160).
  phyc = #DSI_PHYC_DLANE0_ENABLE | #DSI_PHYC_DLANE1_ENABLE | #DSI1_PHYC_CLANE_ENABLE
  phyc = phyc | ((#DSI_LPX - 1) << #DSI1_PHYC_ESC_CLK_LPDT_SHIFT)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF, phyc)

  ; 7. Byte-clock calibration. COMPOSED, NOT READ-MODIFY-WRITTEN - see
  ;    #DSI_CTRL_RUNNING at the top of this file for why a read-back this
  ;    block has never been proven to give is not something to build the
  ;    enable bit on.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_CTRL_OFF, #DSI_CTRL_RUNNING)

  ; 8. The four timeouts, :1145-1152. HS transmit timeout disabled; the
  ;    other three as the driver sets them.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_HSTX_TO_CNT_OFF, 0)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_LPRX_TO_CNT_OFF, $FFFFFF)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_TA_TO_CNT_OFF, 100000)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PR_TO_CNT_OFF, 100000)

  ; 9. DISP1 carries the overflow of a long command payload through the
  ;    pixel FIFO. Enabled even though this bring-up sends nothing long,
  ;    because the driver enables it unconditionally and a half-set-up
  ;    FIFO is a worse thing to leave behind than an idle one.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_DISP1_CTRL_OFF, #DSI1_DISP1_CTRL_VALUE)

  ; 10. The AFE comes out of reset LAST. Everything above is
  ;     configuration; this is the switch.
  ;
  ;     THE COMPOSED VALUE IS WRITTEN, NOT A READ-MODIFY-WRITE, and the
  ;     difference is the whole of the first night's failure. The driver
  ;     does `READ(PHY_AFEC0) & ~RESET` here, which is only safe on a
  ;     register that reads back what was put in it. On this part, after
  ;     that read-modify-write, the register held $00000300 - every bias
  ;     and drive field gone - and the PHY would not start a packet, so
  ;     all two hundred DCS commands timed out at 500 ms each and made
  ;     the board unreachable for a hundred seconds.
  ;
  ;     Writing the value this procedure composed is correct whichever
  ;     way the register actually behaves: if it reads back, this is the
  ;     same word the read-modify-write would have produced; if it does
  ;     not, this is the only way to keep the fields.
  ;
  ;     CLEAR STAT IMMEDIATELY BEFORE THE RELEASE, NOT AFTER IT. That
  ;     ordering is the whole of a measurement defect this file used to
  ;     have, and it is worth spelling out because it produced the
  ;     campaign's headline claim.
  ;
  ;     STAT's lane bits are latched EVENTS - "signalled when the lane
  ;     enters the given state" (vc4_dsi.c:245-252) - and a lane that
  ;     comes up into LP-11 and stays there enters STOP exactly ONCE, at
  ;     the instant the AFE leaves reset. The old code cleared STAT after
  ;     that instant had passed and then waited 20 ms for an edge that
  ;     could no longer happen. An empty STAT was then read as "no lane
  ;     ever reaches STOP" - and a PHY sitting correctly in LP-11 gives
  ;     precisely that reading.
  ;
  ;     So the window is opened first and the release happens inside it.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF))
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_OFF, dsi_afec0_want & ~#DSI1_PHY_AFEC0_RESET)
  delay(20)
  dsi_stat_release = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)

  ; 11. DISP0 in video mode, but NOT enabled. The pixel-clock divider
  ;     field carries the 24/lanes divider, the format is RGB888, and
  ;     LP-STOP happens once per frame.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF, (#DSI_DIVIDER << #DSI_DISP0_PIX_CLK_DIV_SHIFT) | (#DSI_PFORMAT_RGB888 << #DSI_DISP0_PFORMAT_SHIFT) | (#DSI_DISP0_LP_STOP_PERFRAME << #DSI_DISP0_LP_STOP_CTRL_SHIFT) | #DSI_DISP0_ST_END)

  ; 12. THE SECOND WINDOW, KEPT ON PURPOSE, AND IT MEANS THE OPPOSITE.
  ;
  ;     Clear STAT again now that the AFE has been out of reset for
  ;     20 ms, and watch another 20 ms. A lane resting in LP-11 has no
  ;     further transitions to make, so THIS window being empty is the
  ;     EXPECTED reading and says nothing about whether the PHY drives.
  ;
  ;     Both numbers are kept because together they separate two states
  ;     that a single reading cannot: STOP latched in the first window
  ;     and not the second is a PHY that came up and settled, which is
  ;     right; empty in BOTH is a PHY that never drove at all.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF))
  delay(20)
  dsi_stat_settled = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)

  dsi_up = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiUlpsCycle() - COMMAND the lanes into ultra-low-power state and back
;  out, and report what STAT says at each end.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS. Every reading taken so far is passive: the host is
;  configured, the AFE is released, and STAT is watched to see whether
;  anything happens on its own. Nothing does, and two different faults
;  produce that - a transmitter that is not driving, and a transmitter
;  driving LP-11 into a cable that cannot carry it. A passive reading
;  cannot separate them, and neither can another one.
;
;  This asks the PHY a question instead. PHYC's ULPS bits are the only
;  documented way to make a lane change state on demand (vc4_dsi.c:745-
;  800). The sequence is the driver's own, with one deliberate
;  difference: it does NOT consult PHY_AFEC0's LATCH_ULPS bit, which on
;  this part does not read back, so the driver's early-out - which is
;  what makes its ULPS exit dead code here - cannot fire.
;
;    clear STAT's ULPS bits
;    PHYC |= the three ULPS requests
;    wait for STAT's ULPS bits, bounded
;    clear STAT's STOP bits
;    PHYC &= ~the requests
;    wait for STAT's STOP bits, bounded
;
;  WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS, written down before it is run:
;
;    both waits satisfied - the lane state machines respond to a
;      command, so the digital half of the PHY is alive and the fault is
;      downstream of it: the analogue drive, or the cable.
;    ULPS entered, STOP never returns - the PHY drives but will not come
;      back to LP-11, which is a lane held by something external. That
;      is the cable.
;    neither - the lane state machines do not move at all. Nothing
;      outside the SoC can cause that, so it is the PHY, and the
;      write-only bias is the only remaining candidate in it.
;
;  It leaves PHYC exactly as it found it, whichever way it goes: the
;  request bits are removed on every exit path including the failing
;  one, the same way the driver does at :776-780.
;
;  Returns 0, 1 or 2 - how many of the two waits were satisfied.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiUlpsCycle()
  Define phyc.i
  Define t0.i
  Define rc.i

  If dsi_up = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  rc = 0
  dsi_ulps_ran = 1
  phyc = DsiRd(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF)

  ; ---- into ULPS -----------------------------------------------------
  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, #DSI1_STAT_ULPS_2LANE)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF, phyc | #DSI1_PHYC_ULPS_2LANE)

  t0 = millis()
  While (millis() - t0) < 50
    If (DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF) & #DSI1_STAT_ULPS_2LANE) = #DSI1_STAT_ULPS_2LANE
      Break
    EndIf
  Wend
  dsi_ulps_enter = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)
  If (dsi_ulps_enter & #DSI1_STAT_ULPS_2LANE) = #DSI1_STAT_ULPS_2LANE
    rc = 1
  EndIf

  ; ---- back out ------------------------------------------------------
  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, #DSI1_STAT_STOP_2LANE)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF, phyc)

  t0 = millis()
  While (millis() - t0) < 50
    If (DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF) & #DSI1_STAT_STOP_2LANE) = #DSI1_STAT_STOP_2LANE
      Break
    EndIf
  Wend
  dsi_ulps_exit = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)
  If rc = 1 And (dsi_ulps_exit & #DSI1_STAT_STOP_2LANE) = #DSI1_STAT_STOP_2LANE
    rc = 2
  EndIf

  ; PHYC is put back whatever happened - a lane left with a standing
  ; ULPS request would make every later reading a reading of this probe.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF, phyc)

  dsi_ulps_rc = rc
  ProcedureReturn rc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiForceStopCycle() - order data lane 0 into STOP and watch for it.
;
;  WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE PROBE FROM THE ULPS ONE. ULPS is a state the
;  lane negotiates on the wire: both lines are driven low, and although
;  nothing outside the part can prevent a transmitter REPORTING that it
;  entered it, the doubt has been recorded honestly - if STAT's lane bits
;  turn out to be line-state detectors rather than the transmitter's own
;  view, a cable fault could suppress them.
;
;  FORCE_STOP has no such reading. It is an order to one lane's state
;  machine, issued and observed entirely inside the SoC, and vc4 never
;  uses it, so nothing about the far end is involved even in principle.
;  A block that latches the request in PHYC and never reports the state
;  is a block whose lane state machine is not running.
;
;  THE LATCH IS CLEARED BEFORE THE ORDER, NOT AFTER. STAT's lane bits are
;  latched ENTRY events (vc4_dsi.c:245-252), so a clear taken after the
;  edge erases the only evidence the probe exists to collect. That
;  mistake cost this campaign a day and it is written up in
;  [[Recurring bug shapes]] under "the latch was cleared after the edge it
;  was watching for". The order below is: clear, order, wait, read.
;
;  PHYC is restored on every path. Returns 1 if D0_STOP appeared, 0 if it
;  did not, and -1 if the host was not up to ask.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiForceStopCycle()
  Define phyc.i
  Define t0.i

  If dsi_up = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  dsi_fs_ran = 1
  dsi_fs_rc  = 0
  phyc = DsiRd(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF)
  dsi_fs_phyc_was = phyc

  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, #DSI1_STAT_PHY_D0_STOP)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF, phyc | #DSI_PHYC_DLANE0_FORCE_STOP)
  dsi_fs_phyc_back = DsiRd(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF)

  t0 = millis()
  While (millis() - t0) < 50
    If (DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF) & #DSI1_STAT_PHY_D0_STOP) <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
  Wend
  dsi_fs_stat = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)
  If (dsi_fs_stat & #DSI1_STAT_PHY_D0_STOP) <> 0
    dsi_fs_rc = 1
  EndIf

  DsiWr(#DSI1_PHYC_OFF, phyc)
  ProcedureReturn dsi_fs_rc
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiPoke(off, v) - write ONE 32-bit word into the DSI1 block by hand.
;
;  THIS IS AN INSTRUMENT AND IT IS DELIBERATELY BLUNT. `mmio` reads and
;  refuses to write, on the grounds that a monitor command that can put
;  any word at any address turns a wrong keystroke into a dead board.
;  That argument holds for the whole address space and does not hold
;  inside one 140-byte register block whose behaviour is the open
;  question of the campaign.
;
;  It exists because PHY_AFEC0 accepted three of the bits written to it
;  and dropped the rest, and no amount of reading the driver will say
;  whether the others are write-only, unimplemented, or refused. Two
;  pokes and two reads answer it.
;
;  Refuses anything outside 0..$8C or not a multiple of four: past $8C
;  there are no registers, only the identity string, and an unaligned
;  write to a peripheral is not the write anybody meant.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiPoke(off.i, v.i)
  If off < 0 Or off > $8C
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (off & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  DsiWr(off, v)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiVideoOn() / DsiVideoOff() - the last bit, on its own.
;
;  Separate from the bring-up because the panel's DCS init table has to
;  run BETWEEN them: the panel is talked to in escape mode with the
;  video generator stopped, and only then told to show pixels.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DsiVideoOn()
  DsiWr(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF, DsiRd(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF) | #DSI_DISP0_ENABLE)
  dsi_video = 1
EndProcedure

Procedure DsiVideoOff()
  DsiWr(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF, DsiRd(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF) & ~#DSI_DISP0_ENABLE)
  dsi_video = 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE ESCAPE-MODE COMMAND PATH
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiXferReset() - the driver's own recovery from a transfer that never
;  finished (vc4_dsi.c:1394-1400): drop CMD_EN, wait, reset the FIFOs,
;  zero the packet control register.
;
;  It is called on every failing exit rather than only on a timeout, for
;  the reason the I2C library learned the hard way on this same bench: a
;  transfer that stops half way leaves the controller in a state the
;  NEXT command inherits, and then every second reading comes off
;  hardware nobody has put back.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DsiXferReset()
  DsiWr(#DSI1_TXPKT1C_OFF, DsiRd(#DSI1_TXPKT1C_OFF) & ~#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_EN)
  delayMicroseconds(1)
  ; The composed word, not READ(CTRL) | RESET_FIFOS. Same argument as
  ; step 7 of the bring-up: a read-modify-write on a register that has
  ; not been proven to read back can clear DSI_CTRL_EN, and doing that
  ; inside the recovery path would disable the block for every command
  ; after the first failure.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_CTRL_OFF, #DSI_CTRL_REFIFO)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_CTRL_OFF, #DSI_CTRL_RUNNING)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_TXPKT1C_OFF, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiXfer(dt, b0, b1, wantRx) - one SHORT packet, sent and waited for.
;
;  Every command in this panel's init table is one or two bytes, and one
;  status read comes back the same way, so the whole of what this bench
;  needs is the short-packet path: the header carries the data type and
;  both parameter bytes, and neither FIFO is involved at all
;  (vc4_dsi.c:1231-1234).
;
;  LOW POWER MODE IS ALWAYS SET. The panel descriptor asks for
;  MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM (panel-waveshare-dsi-v2.c:2159), so every command
;  goes out in escape mode rather than on the high-speed lanes.
;
;  Returns 1 on completion, 0 otherwise, and leaves the reason in
;  DsiLastInt(). A stall is NOT a hang: every wait here is bounded by
;  both a spin count and a millisecond deadline, because a poll with one
;  bound is a poll that hangs when that bound is the wrong kind.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiXferOnce(dt.i, b0.i, b1.i, wantRx.i)
  Define pkth.i
  Define pktc.i
  Define t0.i
  Define spin.i
  Define st.i
  Define done.i

  dsi_xfers = dsi_xfers + 1

  pkth = (dt & $FF) | (((b0 & $FF) | ((b1 & $FF) << 8)) << #DSI_TXPKT1H_BC_PARAM_SHIFT)

  pktc = #DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_EN | #DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_MODE_LP
  pktc = pktc | (1 << #DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_REPEAT_SHIFT)
  If wantRx <> 0
    pktc = pktc | (#DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_CTRL_RX << #DSI_TXPKT1C_CMD_CTRL_SHIFT)
  EndIf

  ; Clear the two completion bits so what is polled for is this
  ; transfer's and not the previous one's, and clear STAT's copies too -
  ; both are write-one-to-clear and STAT is the one that cannot be
  ; masked away.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_INT_STAT_OFF, #DSI1_INT_XFER_DONE)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, #DSI1_STAT_TXPKT1_DONE | #DSI1_STAT_TXPKT1_END)

  ; ENABLE THE COMPLETION INTERRUPTS EVEN THOUGH NOTHING SERVICES THEM.
  ;
  ; The first version of this file did not, on the reasoning that a
  ; poller does not need an interrupt. That reasoning is only sound if
  ; INT_STAT latches regardless of INT_EN, and on many blocks of this
  ; family it does not - it is the ENABLED interrupts that appear there.
  ; The driver writes INT_EN before every transfer (vc4_dsi.c:1330-1340)
  ; and this now does the same, so the polled register is one the
  ; hardware is actually being asked to fill in.
  ;
  ; $3FC2 is the eight error bits the driver always enables plus
  ; TXPKT1_DONE, and $200000 is PHY_DIR_RTF, which is what completes a
  ; READ. Both are enabled for every transfer rather than choosing
  ; between them, because an interrupt nobody services costs nothing and
  ; a completion nobody can see costs a night.
  DsiWr(#DSI1_INT_EN_OFF, $00203FC2)

  DsiWr(#DSI1_TXPKT1H_OFF, pkth)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_TXPKT1C_OFF, pktc)

  ; ======================================================================
  ;  WHAT COUNTS AS DONE DEPENDS ON WHETHER A REPLY WAS ASKED FOR, AND
  ;  FOR ONE DAY IT DID NOT. THIS IS THE FIX FOR A FALSE POSITIVE ON THE
  ;  MOST IMPORTANT MEASUREMENT IN THE WHOLE DSI CAMPAIGN.
  ;
  ;  This loop used to accept #DSI1_INT_XFER_DONE - TXPKT1_DONE OR
  ;  PHY_DIR_RTF - for EVERY transfer, read or write. TXPKT1_DONE means
  ;  "the packet I sent has gone out". On a read it fires almost at once
  ;  and long before the panel could have turned the bus around, so the
  ;  loop exited on the TRANSMIT, sampled RXPKT1H while it was still
  ;  empty, and handed DsiDcsRead a register full of zeros. DsiDcsRead
  ;  shifted that into a byte and `dsi power` printed
  ;
  ;      Power Mode = 00
  ;      A BYTE CAME BACK FROM THE PANEL.
  ;
  ;  with INT_STAT reading 00000002 - TXPKT1_DONE alone, PHY_DIR_RTF
  ;  (bit 21) and RXPKT1 (bit 4) both clear. Nothing came back at all.
  ;  A read that reports success against a panel that never answered is
  ;  worse than no read: it is the one test that was supposed to be
  ;  impossible to fake.
  ;
  ;  THE DRIVER DOES NOT DO THIS, and it is explicit about it.
  ;  vc4_dsi.c:1327-1336 CHOOSES the completion interrupt by whether a
  ;  reply is expected:
  ;
  ;      if (msg->rx_len) {
  ;              DSI_PORT_WRITE(INT_EN, (DSI1_INTERRUPTS_ALWAYS_ENABLED |
  ;                                      DSI1_INT_PHY_DIR_RTF));
  ;      } else {
  ;              DSI_PORT_WRITE(INT_EN, (DSI1_INTERRUPTS_ALWAYS_ENABLED |
  ;                                      DSI1_INT_TXPKT1_DONE));
  ;      }
  ;
  ;  and only once that completion arrives does it read RXPKT1H at
  ;  :1358-1385. TXPKT1_DONE is never a read's completion there.
  ;
  ;  So the mask is chosen the same way here. The interrupt ENABLES stay
  ;  broad - an interrupt nobody services costs nothing - but what this
  ;  loop WAITS FOR narrows to the one bit that means what is being
  ;  claimed. RXPKT1 (bit 4) is accepted alongside PHY_DIR_RTF because a
  ;  received packet is a received packet however the block announces it.
  ; ======================================================================
  If wantRx <> 0
    done = #DSI1_INT_PHY_DIR_RTF | #DSI1_INT_RXPKT1
  Else
    done = #DSI1_INT_TXPKT1_DONE
  EndIf

  t0 = millis()
  spin = 4000000
  Repeat
    ; POLL BOTH. INT_STAT is what the driver's handler reads, and STAT
    ; is the raw status that no enable mask gates. Either one saying the
    ; packet is done is the packet being done.
    ;
    ; STAT's TXPKT1_DONE is folded in ONLY for a write. On a read it is
    ; the very bit that produced the false positive above, and STAT has
    ; no copy of PHY_DIR_RTF to offer instead.
    st = DsiRd(#DSI1_INT_STAT_OFF)
    dsi_last_stat = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)
    If wantRx = 0 And (dsi_last_stat & #DSI1_STAT_TXPKT1_DONE) <> 0
      st = st | #DSI1_INT_TXPKT1_DONE
    EndIf
    If (st & done) <> 0
      dsi_last_int = st
      DsiWr(#DSI1_INT_STAT_OFF, st)
      If (st & #DSI1_INT_ERROR_BITS) <> 0
        dsi_xfer_fails = dsi_xfer_fails + 1
        DsiXferReset()
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
      If wantRx <> 0
        dsi_last_rxpkt = DsiRd(#DSI1_RXPKT1H_OFF)
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    If (st & #DSI1_INT_ERROR_BITS) <> 0
      dsi_last_int = st
      DsiWr(#DSI1_INT_STAT_OFF, st)
      dsi_xfer_fails = dsi_xfer_fails + 1
      DsiXferReset()
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    spin = spin - 1
  Until spin <= 0 Or (millis() - t0) > #DSI_XFER_MS

  dsi_last_int = DsiRd(#DSI1_INT_STAT_OFF)
  dsi_last_stat = DsiRd(#DSI1_STAT_OFF)
  dsi_xfer_fails = dsi_xfer_fails + 1
  DsiXferReset()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiXfer(dt, b0, b1, wantRx) - one packet, with the REFERENCE DRIVER'S
;  OWN RECOVERY behind it.
;
;  THIS IS THE ONE THING vc4_dsi.c DOES IN THE FAILING PATH THAT THIS
;  FILE DID NOT, and it exists in the driver for exactly the symptom this
;  bench is looking at. vc4_dsi_host_transfer (:1405-1438):
;
;      ret = vc4_dsi_transfer(dsi, msg, false);
;      if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT) {
;              DSI_PORT_WRITE(STAT, STAT_ERR_CONT_LP1);
;              if (!(disp0_ctrl & DSI_DISP0_ENABLE)) {
;                      /* If video mode not enabled, then try recovering
;                       * by enabling it briefly to clear FIFOs and the
;                       * state. */
;                      ...ENABLE, 30 ms, clear, 30 ms, retry once...
;
;  Read that comment again: the driver's authors found that a transfer
;  can time out while DISP0 is not enabled, and that turning video mode
;  on for thirty milliseconds unsticks the block. Every command this
;  bench has sent has been sent with DISP0 not enabled, and every one has
;  timed out with CMD_EN latched and the packet never started. That is
;  the condition the workaround names.
;
;  It is deliberately NOT a loop. One retry, exactly as the driver does,
;  so a link that carries nothing costs a command about a fifth of a
;  second rather than an unbounded number of thirty-millisecond stalls.
;
;  DISP0's ENABLE bit is the only bit touched, and it is put back. That
;  is safe with no pixel source behind it: the pixel valve is not
;  running, so the block has nothing to scan out and simply drains.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiXfer(dt.i, b0.i, b1.i, wantRx.i)
  Define disp0.i

  If DsiXferOnce(dt, b0, b1, wantRx) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; Only the timeout case gets the recovery. An error bit means the link
  ; answered something, and re-driving it would lose the reason.
  If (dsi_last_int & #DSI1_INT_ERROR_BITS) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  DsiWr(#DSI1_STAT_OFF, #DSI1_STAT_ERR_CONT_LP1)

  disp0 = DsiRd(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF)
  If (disp0 & #DSI_DISP0_ENABLE) <> 0
    ; Already in video mode; the driver does not retry in that case
    ; either, it just reports.
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  DsiWr(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF, disp0 | #DSI_DISP0_ENABLE)
  delay(30)
  DsiWr(#DSI1_DISP0_CTRL_OFF, disp0)
  delay(30)

  ProcedureReturn DsiXferOnce(dt, b0, b1, wantRx)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiDcsWrite(cmd, param, nparam) - a DCS write with zero or one
;  parameter, which is every command in this panel's table.
;
;  The data type is chosen the way mipi_dsi_dcs_write does: no parameter
;  is a SHORT_WRITE, one parameter is a SHORT_WRITE_PARAM. Anything
;  longer would be a long packet and this procedure refuses it rather
;  than sending a short packet with the tail missing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiDcsWrite(cmd.i, param.i, nparam.i)
  If nparam = 0
    ProcedureReturn DsiXfer(#MIPI_DSI_DCS_SHORT_WRITE, cmd, 0, 0)
  EndIf
  If nparam = 1
    ProcedureReturn DsiXfer(#MIPI_DSI_DCS_SHORT_WRITE_PARAM, cmd, param, 0)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DsiDcsRead(cmd) - one DCS read, returning 0..255, or -1.
;
;  THIS IS THE MEASUREMENT THE WHOLE STAGE EXISTS FOR. Every write in
;  this file can succeed against a panel that is not there: the host
;  will happily clock a packet into a disconnected ribbon and report
;  TXPKT1_DONE. A read cannot. The bus has to turn around, the panel has
;  to drive it, and the byte that comes back was composed by the part on
;  the other end.
;
;  The reply to a short read arrives in RXPKT1H's SHORT_0 field
;  (vc4_dsi.c:1372-1382), not in the RX FIFO - the FIFO is for long
;  replies. A long reply to a one-byte read is a protocol error here and
;  is reported as one rather than being decoded anyway.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DsiDcsRead(cmd.i)
  Define rx.i

  ; Tell the panel how much it may send back. Without it a panel is
  ; entitled to return its own idea of a maximum and overrun the reply.
  If DsiXfer(#MIPI_DSI_SET_MAX_RETURN_SIZE, 1, 0, 0) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  If DsiXfer(#MIPI_DSI_DCS_READ, cmd, 0, 1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  ; THE COMPLETION MUST HAVE BEEN A READ'S COMPLETION. DsiXferOnce now
  ; only exits a wantRx transfer on PHY_DIR_RTF or RXPKT1, so this can no
  ; longer be false - it is here so that it CANNOT BECOME false again.
  ; The bug this replaces returned $00 from an all-zero RXPKT1H and the
  ; monitor printed "A BYTE CAME BACK FROM THE PANEL"; nothing below
  ; distinguishes a genuine zero byte from an empty register, so the
  ; distinction has to be made from the interrupt that says a packet
  ; arrived, which is what vc4_dsi.c:1327-1358 waits on.
  If (dsi_last_int & (#DSI1_INT_PHY_DIR_RTF | #DSI1_INT_RXPKT1)) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  rx = dsi_last_rxpkt
  If (rx & #DSI_RXPKT1H_ERR_BITS) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If (rx & #DSI_RXPKT1H_PKT_TYPE_LONG) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (rx >> #DSI_RXPKT1H_SHORT_0_SHIFT) & $FF
EndProcedure

